    {
      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "9d3a454ebf08c88eff804008793760ca",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"can-autogluon-beat-the-naive-baselines\">Can AutoGluon beat the naive baselines?</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Same question as <code>mlforecast/07_beat_naive.py</code>, answered with <code>autogluon.timeseries</code>.\nThe bar is <strong>seasonal naive</strong> (same slot yesterday, <code>m=48</code>), not plain naive.</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Data: <code>mlforecast/data/ev_long.parquet</code> (30 synthetic EV charge points, 8 weeks, 30-min).</li>\n<li>Day-ahead framing: forecast 48 slots from each midnight (<code>prediction_length=48</code>).</li>\n<li>The predictor is <strong>fit once</strong> on history before the backtest. Each backtest day is then forecast from\n  its own midnight with the <em>fitted</em> models (no refit), so the scores are rolling-origin out-of-sample.</li>\n<li>AutoGluon's own <code>SeasonalNaive</code> / <code>Naive</code> sit in the same model zoo, so baselines and challengers share\n  one code path. Scores are recomputed in numpy (MAE, RMSE, MASE) so they match the mlforecast notebook.</li>\n</ul></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
